  "body": "**P0 (#630):** lathe posts wrote the insert's **nose-centre** path under G40. With normal touch-off, the controlled point is the imaginary tip (LinuxCNC Lathe User Information §7.1), so turned diameters came out **2r oversize** and faces **r long**. Confirmed against the control vendor's own behaviour: PathPilot's conversational posts the orientation's control point with G40, and refuses a tool whose orientation can't make the cut. That sample is used as behaviour reference only.\n\n## What changed\n- **Tool orientation** (LinuxCNC 1-9 chart: Lathe User Information §3 figure, §7.1, §4.1):\n  - `Tool.orientation: Option<u8>`. Turning inserts default to **2** (OD, cutting toward the chuck), boring bars to **3**.\n  - Editable in the tool editor, which shows the default, through the undoable `ChangeTool`.\n  - The project key is written only when set, so old files load with the default. 1-9 is enforced at command creation and at file read.\n  - Fanuc's tip chart is different, and no post writes either number, so only the LinuxCNC chart is modelled.\n- **One shared conversion,** `crates/post/src/post/nose_tip.rs::control_point_path`, called once from `posting::op_streams` (`posting_nose.rs`), not per .cps:\n  - Every move, rapids and arc centres included, shifts by r·(ox, oz) for the orientation.\n  - This is exact on diameters, faces, tapers and arcs: the cutting path is already the profile offset by r, and the insert is rigid.\n  - Refused by name: a path on two meridians or off every meridian, a C angle, an off-plane arc, and paths carrying cycle, thread, compensation or probe records.\n  - Converted ops: facing, profile rough/finish, chamfer, taper, multi-pass, boring. Grooving, parting, threading, form and drilling post as before.\n- **The cache stays the nose centre.** The verifier, stock sim, gouge laws and CL goldens are unchanged. Orientation is not part of the op stamp, so editing it never stales an op.\n- **Orientation fit, refused by name at post:** a corner tip reaches within 45° of its direction, an axis tip within 90°, and 9 fits anything.\n  - Facing needs −Z and −X (2, 6, 7 fit), OD turning −X, boring +X.\n  - A cut needing −X and +Z gives exactly [1, 5, 6], matching PathPilot's own refusal.\n- **fanuc-lathe.cps:** the header states the control-point rule (G40). The tool note adds `ORIENTATION q` from a new commentary parameter `PARAM ToolOrientation`, documented in CNC-INTERMEDIATE.md.\n- **Decision record:** `docs/decisions/630-nose-tip.md`.\n\n## Golden diffs (Fanuc Lathe; r = 0.8, orientation 2)\n- **`facing.nc`:** every changed line shifts X −1.6 (diameter) and Z −0.8. The last pass now posts the facing plane (Z14, was Z14.8).\n  - Passes end at **X-1.6**: the nose centre still reaches the axis, so the control point stands r past centre. This is a normal facing overtravel, but a machine with a 0 X limit would need #631.\n- **`profile_rough_finish.nc`:** shifts by X −1.6 / Z −0.8. The Ø12 flange posts **X12** (was X13.6), the shoulder Z2 (was Z2.8), and the Ø7 neck X7 (was X8.6). Three points differ by 0.001 from a pure shift because of 1 µm grid rounding.\n- **Unchanged:** groove/part, axial drill, thread G76 and C-axis goldens. In `lathe_post.rs`, the facing assertion now expects `X-1.6 Z14` and `ORIENTATION 2`.\n\n## How tested\n- **`nose_tip` units (7):**\n  - the OD finish posts the finished diameter, and a face posts Z0;\n  - every orientation's offset;\n  - a 30° taper is exact;\n  - arcs keep their radius and move their centre;\n  - the X-meridian case;\n  - refusals;\n  - orientation 9 is identity.\n- **`orientation` units (3),** including PathPilot's [1, 5, 6].\n- **App `lathe_nose_tip` (6):**\n  - a Ø19.05 neck posts X19.05 and the shoulder Z0, while the cache stays nose-centre;\n  - facing reaches the axis;\n  - every fitting orientation equals cache + r·offset, and editing never stales the op;\n  - mismatched orientations are refused;\n  - ChangeTool undo/redo, and code 10 is refused;\n  - a boring bar defaults to 3.\n- **Round-trip and UI:** `project_roundtrip::a_lathe_orientation_round_trips_and_old_tools_take_the_default`, and the UI orientation row.\n- **Gate:**\n  - the non-thread turning and lathe suites, `lathe_post_g76`, conformance, golden_cl, posting, project_roundtrip and commands_undo;\n  - the tool suites, c_axis_live, control_comp, wear_comp, program_entry, inverted_post and coolant_followups;\n  - camcraft-post and camcraft-tools.\n  - Clippy is clean on 5 crates.\n\n## Follow-ups\n- **Grooving and parting blades:** they post the centre of the blade's width, but a blade is touched off on a corner (LinuxCNC §4.2), so a width/2 Z offset is likely owed. Filed separately.\n- **Refusal rule:** checking the leading direction would need insert lead angles, which the tool library doesn't model.\n- **#626 (PathPilot lathe post):** consumes the converted stream once merged; its goldens need regenerating.\n- **#639 (#629):** may hit small conflicts in test `Tool` literals.\n\nChangelog: Lathe posts write the turning tool's control point for its orientation (LinuxCNC 1-9, editable per tool; default 2 for turning inserts, 3 for boring bars) instead of the nose-centre path: a Ø19.05 finish now posts X19.05 and a face at Z0 posts Z0; an orientation that cannot cut the operation is refused by name (#630)\n\nFixes #630\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01CUsqDAgjpiZbXSvmXLvdmL\n",
